<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Northern War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1700">1700</a>–<a href="/years/1721">1721</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Northern War (1700–1721) was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and a coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After early Swedish successes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a>'s defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in 1709 broke Swedish power, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 transferred Baltic provinces to Russia, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> and establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> as a great power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-northern-war.jpg" alt="Great Northern War" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imonoz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Northern_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Between 1560 and 1658 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> created a Baltic empire centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, with the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> it gained territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Western Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> and the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. These gains rested on a well-trained, professional army and on a modernised administration. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> succeeded in 1697, aged 14, he took over the empire as an absolute monarch. Sensing an opportunity under a young king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> formed a coalition against Sweden, and the plans to attack were made at a meeting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II the Strong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> at Rawa Ruska in September 1698.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war began in 1700. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s ally Holstein-Gottorp, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> attacked Swedish Livonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>. Charles XII forced Denmark–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> out of the war in August 1700 by the Peace of Travendal, after the Swedish navy and an Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>. He then turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a>, defeating the Saxon-Polish forces at Kliszów in 1702 and Pultusk in 1703, replacing Augustus with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a> in 1704 and compelling Augustus to accept defeat in the Treaty of Altranstädt in October 1706.</p><p>Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> recovered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>. In 1703 he took Nyen at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> nearby, which cemented Russian access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. Charles then marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the campaign ended in 1709 with the destruction of his main army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Battle of Poltava</a>. Charles fled to the Ottoman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Bender</a>. The anti-Swedish coalition revived, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> joined it. By 1710 the Swedes had been evicted from Tallinn, and by 1714 Russia held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>.</p><p>Charles returned and led two campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, in 1716 and 1718. He was shot dead while besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-fredriksten">Fredriksten</a> on 30 November 1718, and his sister Ulrika Eleonora succeeded him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> still refused peace on Peter's terms, and in July 1719 a Russian fleet landed raiders with nearly 30,000 men on the Swedish east coast, burning towns such as Norrtälje, Södertälje, Nyköping and Norrköping.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war ended Swedish supremacy in northern, central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which a coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had successfully contested. Russia, which had founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1703 and held secure access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, stood in a stronger position at its end in 1721. The Swedish empire built since 1560 did not recover its supremacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Northern War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The navy was the realm’s main strength: Niels Juel defeated the Swedes at the Battle of Køge Bay in 1677, and Peter Tordenskjold won at Dynekilen in 1716 during the <b>Great Northern War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Augustus the Strong’s involvement in the <b>Great Northern War</b> brought a Swedish invasion and the humiliating Treaty of Altranstädt in 1706.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…2 November 1721, when the Senate conferred upon Peter I the titles of Emperor of All Russia, Peter the Great and Father of the Fatherland, following his victory over Sweden in the <b>Great Northern War</b> (1700–1721) and the acquisition of the Baltic provinces by the Treaty of Nystad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The anti-Swedish coalition of the <b>Great Northern War</b>, which had faltered before Poltava, re-formed with renewed confidence, and over the following years Russia overran Livonia, Estonia and Finland while Sweden's German possessions fell to Denmark, Prussia, Saxony and Hanover.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Northern War</b> against Sweden drove further change.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the final land battle of the Finnish campaign of the <b>Great Northern War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-19">19 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
